    slug: "arvada-jefferson-county-hillside-grading",
    title: "Arvada or Jefferson County: Who Permits Hillside Grading?",
    description: "Arvada parcels permit through the city; unincorporated Jefferson County goes through county Planning and Zoning, with its own geotechnical and grading review.",
    h1: "Arvada or Jefferson County: Who Permits Hillside Grading?",
    answer: "On the west side of the metro, the same hillside can face two completely different permit counters. Direct answer: whether a hillside grading project in the Arvada area permits through the City of Arvada or through Jefferson County depends entirely on which side of the city limit line the parcel sits on. Land inside Arvada goes through the city's development review and engineering; land in unincorporated Jefferson County goes through the county's Planning and Zoning with its own grading permits, geotechnical review expectations, and stormwater requirements. The engineering is similar either way, but the portals, fee schedules, reviewers, and timelines are not.\n\nThe first step is jurisdictional, not technical. The county assessor's records and the city's limit maps show whether a parcel is inside Arvada or in unincorporated Jefferson County, and the answer is not always obvious: county islands, parcels annexed in phases, and lots along the boundary can surprise owners. Designing to the wrong jurisdiction's standards is one of the most expensive mistakes in hillside work, because each AHJ has its own submittal checklists, review sequences, and inspection scheduling. The engineer confirms the AHJ before scoping the geotechnical investigation.\n\nThe geology driving the review is the Front Range foothills transition: the Dakota Hogback's tilted sandstone ridges, claystone and shale slopes, and colluvial soils that move when saturated. Hillside grading here means cuts and fills on slopes where stability, drainage, and erosion control all interact. Both jurisdictions expect a geotechnical report addressing slope stability under static and seismic conditions, bearing and lateral pressures for retaining walls, and drainage recommendations; both require grading plans showing existing and proposed contours, cut and fill quantities, and erosion and sediment control. Where they differ is process: Arvada routes grading through city engineering review tied to its development permits, while Jefferson County issues its own grading permits with county-specific stormwater and defensible-space expectations for foothill parcels.\n\nRetaining walls concentrate the review. Walls above each jurisdiction's height threshold need engineered designs with structural calculations, and walls supporting structures or surcharges get extra scrutiny for global stability. Drainage design must prove the project does not increase runoff rates or redirect water onto neighboring parcels, a common correction on hillside submittals. The AHJ decides what is approved in both paths.",
    directAnswer: "Hillside grading inside Arvada city limits permits through the City of Arvada's development and engineering review; in unincorporated Jefferson County it goes through the county's Planning and Zoning with county grading permits and stormwater requirements. Confirm the parcel's jurisdiction first, then design to that AHJ's checklists, since the portals, fees, and timelines differ.",
