    h1: "How Are Aspen Roofs Engineered for Mountain Snow Loads?",
    answer: "Aspen roofs are engineered for a snow climate that has little in common with the Front Range plains. Direct answer: ground snow loads in the Aspen area run far above plains values and climb steeply with elevation, so roofs are designed under ASCE 7 for balanced snow plus the unbalanced, drift, and sliding-snow cases that govern real mountain roofs. Pitkin County and the City of Aspen adopt local snow-load provisions and elevation-driven requirements, and the structural engineer confirms the site-specific ground snow load, drift surcharges at steps and parapets, and sliding-snow hazards before sizing a single member.\n\nElevation is the whole story. Moist Pacific storms wring out against the Elk Range, and every thousand feet of elevation adds substantially to the ground snow load; what is a moderate load in the valley becomes an enormous one at the upper ends of Aspen's buildable slopes. The code's balanced load, uniform snow across the roof, is only the starting point. Wind scours snow from windward slopes and piles it on leeward ones, the unbalanced case, and it drifts snow against parapets, at roof steps where an upper roof meets a lower one, around rooftop equipment, and in valleys, where drift surcharges can multiply the local design load several times over the uniform value. The engineer maps every drift-prone geometry on the roof plan and designs the structure beneath for the piled-up load.\n\nSliding snow is the mountain-specific hazard that injures people. Snowpack on steep upper roofs releases and avalanches onto lower roofs, decks, entries, and walkways, delivering impact loads the lower structure must be designed for and creating a life-safety problem at grade. Designers manage it with snow-retention devices that hold the pack in place, with setbacks that keep entries and walkways out of the slide path, and with lower roofs engineered for the impact where sliding cannot be prevented. Where sliding snow threatens pedestrian areas, the civil and architectural design keeps people out of the fall zone.\n\nThe thermal design matters as much as the structural. Heat leaking through the roof melts snow from below, water runs to the cold eave and refreezes into ice dams that back water under the roofing; the defense is a cold roof, generous insulation, meticulous air sealing, and ventilation that keeps the roof deck near outdoor temperature. Low-slope sections get checked for ponding under snowmelt, and deflection limits are held tight because a sagging roof ponds more. Pitkin County's building department reviews the structural calculations against the local snow provisions, and the AHJ decides what is approved.",
    directAnswer: "Aspen roofs are designed for elevation-driven ground snow loads far above plains values, with ASCE 7 balanced, unbalanced, drift, and sliding-snow cases all checked. Engineers map drift surcharges at parapets, steps, and equipment, manage sliding snow with retention devices and setbacks, and use cold-roof assemblies with insulation and ventilation to prevent ice dams.",
