    slug: "boulder-county-vs-city-permitting",
    title: "City of Boulder or Boulder County: Which Permitting Path Applies?",
    description: "Inside Boulder city limits you permit through the city's Planning and Development Services; unincorporated land follows the county's Land Use rules and WUI overlays.",
    h1: "City of Boulder or Boulder County: Which Permitting Path Applies?",
    answer: "Boulder's permit counter depends on which side of an invisible line the parcel sits on. Direct answer: property inside the City of Boulder permits through the city's Planning and Development Services, with its own design review, famously strict energy code, and historic preservation program; property in unincorporated Boulder County permits through the county's Community Planning and Permitting, under the Land Use Code with Site Plan Review for new homes, wildfire mitigation requirements for the WUI zones, and county grading standards. The parcel's jurisdiction, confirmed through the assessor's records, determines the portal, the reviewers, the fee schedule, and the timeline.\n\nThe city's system is design-intensive. Boulder's Planning and Development Services combines planning review, building permits, and inspections, and projects can face design review boards, site review for larger developments, and one of the nation's most demanding local energy codes, which pushes projects toward high-performance envelopes and electrification. Historic landmark and historic district properties add another review layer before any exterior work. The city's process rewards early pre-application meetings, where staff flag the full stack of applicable reviews before the design is finalized.\n\nThe county's system is land-use intensive. In unincorporated Boulder County, new homes and major additions go through Site Plan Review under the Land Use Code, which evaluates siting, grading, access, and compatibility alongside the building plans. Wildfire mitigation under the code's wildfire provisions applies in the mapped Wildfire Zones, extended after the Marshall Fire, requiring ignition-resistant construction details and defensible space. Grading is reviewed for slope stability and drainage, and many foothill parcels use on-site wastewater systems reviewed through the county's public-health process rather than city sewer.\n\nEdge cases keep both agencies busy. County islands, unincorporated parcels surrounded by the city, follow county rules until annexation; properties along the boundary need a definitive jurisdiction check because designing to the wrong standards wastes months. Some addresses with a Boulder mailing address sit entirely in the county. Either way, the AHJ decides what is approved, and its current amendments, energy, wildfire, and grading provisions, set the real requirements.",
    directAnswer: "Inside Boulder city limits, projects permit through the city's Planning and Development Services with strict energy code and design review; in unincorporated Boulder County they go through Community Planning and Permitting under the Land Use Code, with Site Plan Review, wildfire mitigation in mapped zones, and county grading standards. Confirm jurisdiction via assessor records before designing.",
