        heading: "Two Review Systems Side by Side",
        body: "The City of Boulder runs a consolidated review: planning, building, and inspection staff work under one department, and the submittal moves through zoning compliance, building plan review, and specialty reviews such as energy and historic preservation. The city's energy code is the headline difference: it is among the strictest local codes in the country, and projects budget for the envelope, mechanical, and documentation work it demands from the start. Design review adds a discretionary layer for visible projects, where boards weigh compatibility and community character alongside code compliance.\n\nBoulder County's Community Planning and Permitting runs the parallel universe for unincorporated land. Site Plan Review is the county's signature process for new homes, examining the house in its landscape: setbacks, height, grading, driveway access, and wildfire mitigation. The Land Use Code carries the wildfire provisions that hardened post-Marshall Fire construction, and grading review focuses on the foothill realities of steep slopes and drainage. Timelines, fees, and submittal portals are entirely separate from the city's, and county reviewers bring deep familiarity with mountain and foothill construction that shapes their corrections.",
